.TH OBSERVER-DASHBOARD 1 "2026-08-27" "observer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
observer-dashboard \- watch a machine's CPU and memory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B observer-dashboard
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B observer-dashboard
opens a window that polls an Observer service once a second and shows what it
reports.
.PP
With no configuration it talks to the service on this machine over the local
socket, and needs no token. For that to work your account has to be in the
.B observer
group:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo usermod \-aG observer $USER
.RE
.PP
then log out and back in.
.PP
It can point at a service on another computer instead, and that is what makes
watching several machines nearly free. A remote service does require a token:
get it by running
.B observer share
on that machine.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B Observer__BaseAddress
The service to query, for example
.IR http://other-machine:5057/ .
Defaults to the local socket.
.TP
.B Observer__ApiToken
The token for a remote service. Not needed for this machine.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/Observer/client.json
Read when the environment variables are absent. Recognised fields are
.B baseAddress
and
.BR apiToken .
It carries another machine's credential, so it is not something to share.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR observer (1)
